1
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:07,820
In 1899, two lawyers from
Chattanooga, Tennessee, came to see

2
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:12,370
him. Benjamin Thomas and Joseph
Whitehead had an idea: put

3
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:14,369
Coca-Cola in bottles.

4
00:00:14,369 --> 00:00:16,194
Candler wasn't impressed.

5
00:00:16,194 --> 00:00:19,219
Bottling was messy, and his
drink was made for the soda

6
00:00:19,219 --> 00:00:22,294
fountain. But if they wanted
to take the risk...

7
00:00:22,294 --> 00:00:23,181
fine.

8
00:00:23,181 --> 00:00:26,706
He sold them the rights to bottle
Coca-Cola across most of the

9
00:00:26,706 --> 00:00:29,481
United States. For one dollar.

10
00:00:29,481 --> 00:00:32,501
And, reportedly, he never
even collected it.

11
00:00:32,501 --> 00:00:34,639
That dollar built the empire.

12
00:00:34,639 --> 00:00:38,314
Thomas and Whitehead didn't build
every plant themselves.

13
00:00:38,314 --> 00:00:41,801
They signed up local businessmen who
paid for their own bottling

14
00:00:41,801 --> 00:00:45,549
plants, their own trucks and
their own salesmen.

15
00:00:45,549 --> 00:00:49,474
Town after town got its own
Coca-Cola bottler, with its own

16
00:00:49,474 --> 00:00:53,112
money on the line. The
company sold the syrup.

17
00:00:53,112 --> 00:00:55,132
The bottlers did the rest.

18
00:00:55,132 --> 00:00:57,544
But success attracts copycats.

19
00:00:57,544 --> 00:01:01,095
Soon there were drinks with almost
the same name, in almost the

20
00:01:01,095 --> 00:01:02,360
same bottle.

21
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:06,910
So in 1915, the bottlers asked
glass makers for a bottle you

22
00:01:06,910 --> 00:01:08,873
could recognize in the dark...

23
00:01:08,873 --> 00:01:11,047
or even broken on the ground.

24
00:01:11,047 --> 00:01:15,060
A glass company in Terre Haute,
Indiana, came back with a curvy

25
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:18,040
design. The contour bottle.
